anced  healthcare infrastructure how do you  will you get the medicines from from  fizers to the people who really need  them because if they're in a warehouse  they don't do any good this is a very  big issue and in fact that issue became  very visible during the pandemic  right now there are hundreds of millions  of  doses of our vaccine that are offered  for free not at cost for free including  the shipment and those countries they  have very low vaccination rates they  don't accept any more shipments because  they don't have the infrastructure to do  the vaccinations to  to convince the people to come to do the  to take the vaccines for this reason  in addition to just providing the  medicinal cost we selected five of these  five 45 countries which are  rwanda uganda  ghana malawi and  senegal that we already sign letters  with this governance that we are going  not only to provide the medicines but  work on the ground so to help identify  the blockages the issues because one  thing it is to have a breast cancer  medicine for a woman  but the other thing is that you need to  diagnose that this woman has breast  cancer and one thing is that you are  avoiding to make diagnosis because you  don't have medicines but now you do have  so we need to organize this part as well  the pandemic do you think it is um  so much of this flows from your  learnings with panic do you think the  pandemic has perhaps  reminded the world of the power of  medicine the power of science has it  reminded  your industry of its responsibility to  the whole world rather than just just  those rich countries that are lucky  enough to be able to afford great  medicines i think both are true to a  very high degree i think the world  realized  the value that the vibrant life sciences  sector can bring to society and the life  sciences sector is composed by the  private but also the academia as we saw  the example astrazeneca oxford for  example  so we saw other examples us with biotech  for example that was with a biotech  company and  but also reminded of us when we have  these  weapons in  our  these tools  we must find ways that we  they reach  all and not only those that they can  afford them um can i just quickly ask  you about covid uh  you may well have said this publicly  what are your projections for vaccine  production  for maintaining  control on the pandemic and also the  prospect of new variants and the  requirement to  develop the vaccine i have no concerns  on manufacturing capacity we have built  manufacturing capacity that covers the  needs of the entire world easily right  now and we have perfect our  manufacturing processes now for  clearly we are watching the variants  that are emerging and we we see that  some of them they are smarter than  others and they can evade  to somehow the protection for vaccines  that's why we are working on updated  versions of those vaccines that will  cover those right now we have most of  the data in-house very few remain to to  be collected and then we will present to  regulators like the uk regulator the  european the american regulator all this  information so upgraded and better  vaccines in the future do you have any  idea how many years  we will be administering covert vaccines  do you have a horizon on this i don't  have a crystal ball but there are a lot  of variants that can affect this outcome  but i think the very the virus will  remain among us  and i think we will have the tools to go  back to normal life so i don't think  that because the variants exist we we  have to leave restricts to impose  restrictions on our lives but people who  need to get vaccinated  and then of course there are treatments  the big difference between this year and  last year is that last year we didn't  have that many treatments and if we had  were only for people that were really in  the hospital now we have treatments that  you can take it and don't go to the  hospitals no one should die from covet  nowadays can y